{"id":"move-across-outlines","parent":"feat-mcp","ord":"aE","title":"A node cannot move between outlines — the ops can't express a cross-file move","done":"2026-08-25T17:57:17-04:00","desc":"`move_node` deliberately never crosses files (\"trash_node is what does\") — but trash-then-recreate loses the ids: ids are unique across the set INCLUDING Trash, so the recreated nodes get new ids and every inbound reference (see, after, prop refs, mirrors, prop:key lookups by id) silently detaches. The workaround used today: recreate with new ids, trash the old subtree, fix referrers by hand — exactly the multi-step, reference-breaking dance a single op should own. Shape: either `move_node` gains a `file` target (placement moves, id keeps, references keep resolving — the same promise trash already makes about ids moving with nodes), or a dedicated `move_outline` op. The ops-only law names this pattern: an inexpressible ledger fact is a bug to file, and this is it filed.","created":"2026-08-25T16:28:35-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T17:57:21-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/mao.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T16:39:00-04:00","from":"the supervision.olai extraction, 2026-08-25 — moving one subtree out of agents.olai took recreate-under-NEW-ids + trash, because move_node is same-file by design and the duplicate-id fence counts Trash, so same-id recreation is refused","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/3658caf39989ac37e6e9a7de231b82a23218007e","merged":"2026-08-25T18:00:00-04:00","worktree":".worktrees/move-across-outlines"}}
